# 8. FORENSIC ANALYSIS ENGINE

Implement a shared Python processing engine used by both operating modes.

## 8.1 Chromium parser

Analyze compatible Chromium-based browser History databases.

Initially extract supported:
- URLs
- Page titles
- Visit timestamps
- Visit identifiers
- Visit transition metadata where available
- Download metadata supported by the schema

Support Chrome, Edge, Brave, and Chromium only where their database schemas have been verified as compatible.

Inspect tables and columns before querying. Record unsupported or missing schema elements.

## 8.2 Firefox parser

Analyze supported Firefox `places.sqlite` databases.

Initially extract:
- Browsing history
- URLs and titles where available
- Visit timestamps
- Visit identifiers
- Bookmarks
- Related supported metadata

Support only structures actually tested.

## 8.3 Source traceability

Each extracted event should identify:
- Event ID
- Case ID
- Acquisition ID
- Evidence ID
- Analysis run ID
- Browser family
- Event type
- URL where relevant
- Title where relevant
- Raw timestamp
- Timestamp format
- Normalized UTC timestamp
- Timestamp conversion status
- Source database
- Source table
- Source record ID where available
- Parser name and version
- Warnings

Do not invent source record IDs when the source does not provide them.

## 8.4 Timestamp normalization

Implement browser-specific timestamp converters.

Preserve the original timestamp value and its format. Convert supported values to UTC only when the applicable interpretation is known.

Handle:
- Invalid timestamps
- Out-of-range timestamps
- Missing timestamps
- Precision differences
- Ambiguous time formats
- Unsupported timestamp representations

Do not assume that all browser timestamps use the Unix epoch.

## 8.5 Analysis runs

Each analysis must create a separate AnalysisRun.

Record:
- Analysis run ID
- Acquisition ID
- Parser versions
- Start and completion timestamps
- Status
- Records seen
- Records extracted
- Records skipped
- Warnings
- Errors

Repeated analysis must not silently overwrite earlier evidence or results.

Do not collapse separate acquisitions simply because their files have identical hashes.

